                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[i]The Silver Case[/i] is pretty interesting. At times I wish it was strictly a visual novel, but it's clear the studio was experimenting with what was possible with their limited budget and time. I can't fault them for trying an unusual blend of gameplay.\n\nI particularly enjoyed the story's exploration of the anxieties of living in modern society, especially its focus on the dangers of the Internet. Considering that this game was written in the late '90s, it feels forward-thinking.\n\nI love the mixed media approach to telling the story. While most of the game has the same general format, every now and then we get little pieces of shot-on-video movies and even traditional animation. It helps keep the whole thing fresh. [b]Arguably the aesthetics of [i]The Silver Case[/i] are as important (if not more so) as the plot itself.[/b]\n\nThe story can sometimes veer off into weird tangents, only for it to later circle back and re-contextualize things. It's the kind of tale that left me pondering about it after it concluded.\n\n[i]killer7[/i] is one of my favorite games. In [i]The Silver Case[/i], we occasionally get glimpses of the weirdness of Suda51's future works. I don't think this game is as strong as [i]killer7[/i], but it's hardly a fair comparison considering that this one came out much earlier in Suda51's career.\n\nI should note that the game works fine on Steam Deck, but I occasionally had to wait for long stretches while the game loaded the next scene. Perhaps I'm using the wrong compatibility layer? Despite this glitch, it didn't stop me from completing the game.\n\nThis version of the game preserves everything about the original PS1 title, including a control scheme that feels quaint. Players should definitely play the demo first to make sure that they're willing to meet the game where it's at. Recommended for those who enjoy Suda51's other work.",
